<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Repo-scope NuGet config. It defines no package sources of its own — only
  which sources are allowed to serve which package ids.

  It used to define https://nuget.pkg.github.com/charles8051/index.json here.
  That source requires authentication, and an unauthenticated restore does not
  merely skip it — NuGet retries, then fails the whole restore:

    error NU1301: Failed to retrieve information about 'Periphery' from remote
    source 'https://nuget.pkg.github.com/charles8051/download/periphery/index.json'.
      Response status code does not indicate success: 401 (Unauthorized).

  So a source that only the maintainer can read cannot live in a file that
  ships to everyone. It is added at runtime by the workflow that needs it,
  which is also where the credential lives.

  Intentionally NO <clear /> — that would cut off nuget.org itself on a
  machine that inherits it from user scope rather than a machine default, and
  it would cut off local-feed (~/.nuget/local-feed), which cross-repo
  iteration depends on.
-->
<configuration>
  <!--
    Package source mapping. Two things about it are easy to get wrong and are
    the reason this section is spelled out rather than deleted.

    It is EXCLUSIVE. Once a pattern matches, only the sources listing that
    pattern are considered; the rest are not consulted at all, and the error
    says so:

      error NU1100: Unable to resolve 'Periphery (>= 1.0.0-alpha)'.
      PackageSourceMapping is enabled, the following source(s) were not
      considered: nuget.org.

    And the MOST SPECIFIC pattern wins, so nuget.org's `*` does not cover
    `Periphery*` — nuget.org has to list `Periphery*` explicitly to be
    consulted for it. Without that line, a public clone cannot restore
    Periphery even though it is published on nuget.org.

    A source named here but not defined anywhere is skipped silently rather
    than erroring, which is what makes local-feed and github-charles8051
    harmless entries for anyone who has neither.
  -->
  <packageSourceMapping>
    <!--
      Maintainer-only, defined in user scope: a rebuild of a sibling repo is
      picked up on the next restore without cross-repo ProjectReference
      plumbing. NuGet consults every mapped source and takes the highest
      version, so locally that is the just-built package.
    -->
    <packageSource key="local-feed">
      <package pattern="Periphery*" />
      <package pattern="FrameFlow.*" />
    </packageSource>

    <!--
      GitHub Packages, added at runtime by .github/workflows/publish.yml and
      .github/workflows/build.yml, both of which authenticate first. Still
      needed for FrameFlow.Native.Runtime, which has never been published to
      nuget.org — the six projects that reference it cannot restore from a
      public clone until it is. Periphery no longer needs it: 4.1.0-alpha.2 is
      on nuget.org.
    -->
    <packageSource key="github-charles8051">
      <package pattern="Periphery*" />
      <package pattern="FrameFlow.*" />
    </packageSource>

    <!--
      Added at runtime by the build and publish workflows, which pack
      FrameFlow.Native.Runtime BEFORE the solution restores so the six projects
      referencing it resolve the version being built rather than one that exists
      only in a private feed.

      It has to be mapped, not merely added. Mapping is EXCLUSIVE: a source that
      is added but not mapped is never consulted for a pattern another source
      claims, so `dotnet nuget add source` alone would not be enough.

      Undefined outside those jobs, and an undefined mapped source is skipped
      silently, so this entry is inert for everyone else.
    -->
    <packageSource key="bootstrap">
      <package pattern="FrameFlow.*" />
    </packageSource>

    <!--
      Exact ids, not `Periphery*`, and no `FrameFlow.*` at all. Both omissions
      are deliberate: a pattern mapped to BOTH a private source and nuget.org is
      a dependency-confusion hole, because NuGet consults every mapped source
      and takes the highest version. Whoever registers the id on nuget.org
      first wins.

      Every frameflow.* id is unregistered on nuget.org today (checked: 404 for
      frameflow, frameflow.native.runtime, frameflow.media, frameflow.graph,
      frameflow.playback), so mapping `FrameFlow.*` here would let anyone
      displace FrameFlow.Native.Runtime in a CI or release restore — its
      `0.1.1-alpha.*` range floats, so a squatted higher prerelease wins
      outright. It buys nothing in exchange: no FrameFlow package is on
      nuget.org yet.

      `Periphery` and `Periphery.Camera` are named exactly because those two
      are published and owned. The prefix form would have covered unpublished
      sibling ids too — Periphery.Hid and Periphery.Bootloader exist, and
      anything else under that prefix is unclaimed.

      When a FrameFlow package does reach nuget.org, add its exact id here.
      Do not widen this back to a prefix.

      The `*` below does NOT re-open the hole, and this reads like it should, so
      the evidence is here rather than left to be re-derived. Mapping is decided
      by the single most specific matching pattern, and only the sources
      declaring THAT pattern are consulted — a broader pattern on another source
      is not a fallback. Demonstrated on a package that certainly exists:
      map `System.Text.*` to a source that is not defined, leave `*` on
      nuget.org, and ask for System.Text.Json 9.0.0.

        error NU1100: Unable to resolve 'System.Text.Json (>= 9.0.0)' for
        'net10.0'. PackageSourceMapping is enabled, the following source(s) were
        not considered: nuget.org.

      Newtonsoft.Json in the same restore resolved fine through `*`. The same
      shows up in this repo: a public-clone restore reports nuget.org "not
      considered" for FrameFlow.Native.Runtime, with `*` mapped exactly as below.
    -->
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
      <package pattern="Periphery" />
      <package pattern="Periphery.Camera" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>
</configuration>
